## Kafka Guide

Since this project incorporates Kafka as a Pub/Sub service, you must adhere to the following instructions before running the app.  
A `docker-compose.yml` file is included in this project which will be used for creating a Kafka container. Some commands are also added to the `Makefile` file for running a container (The following guide is applicable if GNU/Make tool is installed on your machine).

### Run A Kafka Container

First, make sure Docker is up and running on your machine. Then in the root path of the project run the following command:

```text
$ make docker-up
```

In order to stop the container, you can run the following command:

```text
$ make docker-down
```

## Produce A New Message

By running the following command in the terminal, open the Kafka CLI:

```text
$ docker exec -it kafka sh
```

In the Kafka CLI, consume message from a topic using the following command. For example, if you want to consume data coming from a topic named `testing`, you need to run the following command:

```text
$ kafka-console-consumer.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --topic testing
```

The generated worker is a single long-running process. Its `main` opens the broker, publishes one sample message on startup, and then consumes every message on the topic until you stop it with Ctrl+C:

```go
broker, err := kafka.New("testing", logger)
if err != nil {
	logger.Fatal(err)
}
defer func() { _ = broker.Close() }()

// Publish a sample message. The domain entity carries no wire-format tags, so
// the composition root maps it to a transport payload before marshalling.
sample, _ := json.Marshal(struct {
	Data string `json:"data"`
}{Data: "Hello World"})
_ = broker.Produce(ctx, sample)

// Consume every message until the process is stopped.
go broker.Consume(ctx, func(value []byte) {
	logger.Info("message received", "payload", string(value))
})
```

We use the same topic name (`testing`) as the CLI consumer above. Running `make run` publishes `{"data":"Hello World"}` to the topic and then keeps running, logging any further messages:

```text
{"data":"Hello World"}
```

## Send More Messages

The worker already consumes the topic, so to send more messages, open the Kafka CLI producer:

```text
$ kafka-console-producer.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --topic testing
```

Then enter a message such as:

```text
>Hello World
```

Hitting enter, the terminal window running the worker logs the payload:

```text
message received payload=Hello World
```

## Producer-Only and Consumer-Only Workers

To specialize a worker, keep only the half you need in `main`: delete the `broker.Consume(...)` goroutine for a pure producer, or delete the `broker.Produce(...)` call for a pure consumer. You can also scaffold two separate workers:

```text
$ gopamin new -n producer -t worker -b kafka -l {{.Logger}}
$ gopamin new -n consumer -t worker -b kafka -l {{.Logger}}
```

Edit each to keep just its half, then run both with `make run` (start the consumer first so it doesn't miss the producer's message). The consumer logs each payload it receives:

```text
$ make run
message received payload={"data":"Hello World"}
```
